json -> /tmp/dot1x1304/T33354-B.json
RESULT T33354-B-WITH-EAP-Notification: 1/1 supplicant(s) matched their expectation -- MATCH
                                                 <-- frame 4 is the Notification, on the
                                                     supplicant's own wire
                                                 <-- frame 5 is the supplicant's empty
                                                     reply, which RFC 3748 section 5.2
                                                     requires

Server side:
15:05:11 radeap listening on 0.0.0.0:11812  secret=<12 chars>  clients=10.38.22.34  users=cknotify
15:05:11 notification: 'ck-T33354 notification: password expires in 3 days'
15:05:30 Access-Request id=5 user='cknotify'  EAP code=2(Response) id=1 type=Identity len=8
15:05:30   -> Access-Challenge  EAP-Request/Notification id=2 text='ck-T33354 notification: password expires in 3 days'
15:05:30 Access-Request id=6 user='cknotify'  EAP code=2(Response) id=2 type=Notification len=0
15:05:30   <- the supplicant ANSWERED the notification (data=0 bytes, RFC 3748 5.2 says it carries none)
15:05:30   -> Access-Challenge  EAP-Request/MD5-Challenge id=3
15:05:30 Access-Request id=7 user='cknotify'  EAP code=2(Response) id=3 type=MD5-Challenge len=25
15:05:30   -> Access-Accept + EAP-Success for 'cknotify'
                                                 <-- the reply came back to the server,
                                                     so the DUT relays in BOTH directions
  => STEP B PASS: 8 frames against the control's 6, and the two extra frames are exactly
     the Notification and its reply.  Four things are proved.  First, the DUT passes the
     Notification to the supplicant.  Second, the text is unchanged: the raw bytes
     636b2d...64617973 decode to "ck-T33354 notification: password expires in 3 days",
     the exact string given to the server, 50 bytes in and 50 bytes out.  Third, the
     supplicant's empty reply reached the server, which logged it, so the DUT relays in
     both directions.  Fourth, the notification changed nothing: the exchange went on to
     the MD5 challenge and ended in EAP-Success in 1.04 s, the same as the control.

